[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
NovelClanker is a cross-platform desktop application for planning and writing novels with help from local language models. This specification records what the application must do, as built on the ``prose-writer`` branch, and what is planned next.
<<<

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Introduction

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The scope is the desktop application: its manuscript editor, novel storage, synopsis and plan workflow, prose writer, and its integration with an LM Studio server. LM Studio itself, the models it serves and the operating systems are outside the scope.
<<<

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
.. list-table::
   :header-rows: 1

   * - Term
     - Meaning
   * - Novel
     - One book, stored as a RocksDB database folder named ``*.novel``
   * - Act, chapter, scene
     - The manuscript hierarchy; only scenes hold prose text. Act-level parts may also be called stages, parts or books
   * - Brief
     - A short description of what an act, chapter or scene is for, usually taken from a plan
   * - Synopsis
     - The author's summary of the story, stored exactly as written
   * - Plan
     - A Markdown outline of the novel; a novel can hold several
   * - Plan template
     - The structural model a plan is generated in, such as three acts or inner stages
   * - Planner model
     - The model that generates and refines plans
   * - Prose writer model
     - The model that writes and expands scene text
   * - Passage
     - Text the author selects in a scene (or the whole scene) to be expanded
   * - LM Studio
     - The local model server the application talks to
<<<

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Each requirement has an ID of the form ``AREA-n`` (for example ``FR-PLN-3``), a priority and a status.
<<<

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
- **Must**: required for the product to be usable. **Should**: important, can follow later. **Could**: desirable.
- **Implemented**: present and covered by tests unless noted. **Planned**: agreed direction, not built yet.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Product overview

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The author writes a synopsis, a local planner model turns it into a plan, the author refines the plan by editing it or chatting with the model, and the application builds the manuscript's acts, chapters and scenes from it. The author then writes each scene with its brief in view, either by hand or with the prose writer model: writing a whole scene from its brief, or expanding their own short draft into a longer passage in steps.
<<<

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
::

   Synopsis --Generate Plan--> Plan <--edit / chat--> (refined plan) --Create Structure--> Acts / chapters / scenes
                                                                                                 |
                                                        Write Scene (from brief) / Expand (draft) v
                                                                                            Scene text
<<<

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Plans sit at the centre: each one is refined in a loop of edits and chat before Create Structure turns it into the manuscript.
<<<

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
**Users.** A single author working alone on one machine, who runs LM Studio locally (or on their own network) and owns the models it serves. No accounts, sharing or collaboration.
<<<

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
**Operating environment.**
<<<

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
.. list-table::
   :header-rows: 1

   * - Item
     - Requirement
   * - Operating systems
     - Windows 10 and later (primary, tested); Linux and macOS (supported by the build, untested)
   * - Toolkit
     - Qt 6.8 LTS (Core, Gui, Widgets, Network), C++20
   * - Storage
     - RocksDB 11.1 with LZ4 compression, one database folder per novel
   * - AI server
     - LM Studio with its local server enabled, default ``http://127.0.0.1:1234``
   * - Build
     - Conan 2, CMake 3.23 or later, Ninja; MSVC 19.5 on Windows
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Functional requirements

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
All functional requirements below are implemented; planned functions are listed under Open issues.
<<<

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Novels

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-NOV-1
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
The user can create a novel at a new ``*.novel`` path; an existing path is refused. A new novel starts with Act 1 / Chapter 1 / Scene 1.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-NOV-2
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
The user can open a novel by selecting its folder. A folder that is not a novel is refused without any file being written to it.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-NOV-3
PRIORITY: Should
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
The last novel reopens on startup; a novel path given on the command line is opened instead.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-NOV-4
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Only one application instance can open a novel at a time; a second attempt reports why it failed.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-NOV-5
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Novels can live in folders with non-ASCII names on every platform.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Manuscript structure

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-STR-1
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
The Manuscript tab shows the novel as a tree of acts, chapters and scenes, acts in bold.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-STR-2
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
**+ Act**, **+ Chapter** and **+ Scene** buttons above the tree add a node after the current one, creating missing parents (+ Scene on an empty act also adds a chapter).
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-STR-3
PRIORITY: Should
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
New nodes get default titles (Act N, Chapter N numbered through the book, Scene N); new acts and chapters open for renaming.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-STR-4
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Nodes are renamed by double-click or F2; an empty name is rejected and the old one restored.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-STR-5
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Delete (Del) asks for confirmation, stating how many chapters and scenes go with it, and removes everything below the node.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-STR-6
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Nodes are reordered with Alt+Up/Down or by drag and drop; scenes move between chapters and chapters between acts; drops that break the hierarchy are refused.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-STR-7
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Selecting an act, chapter or scene shows its brief above the editor.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-STR-8
PRIORITY: Could
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
The tree keeps each node's collapsed or expanded state while the novel is open.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Scene editing

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-SCN-1
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Selecting a scene opens its text in a plain-text editor with live Markdown highlighting (headings, emphasis, code, quotes, lists, rules, links).
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-SCN-2
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Text saves automatically 1.5 s after typing stops, when switching scenes, and on exit; Ctrl+S saves at once.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-SCN-3
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Text is stored exactly as shown in the editor, including non-breaking spaces.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-SCN-4
PRIORITY: Should
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Each scene has its own undo history.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-SCN-5
PRIORITY: Should
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
The status bar shows word counts for the scene, its chapter and the novel, and whether the scene is saved.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-SCN-6
PRIORITY: Should
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Undo, Redo, Cut, Copy and Paste in the Edit menu act on whichever editor has focus, and do nothing in an editor that is locked while a model writes.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Prose writer

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-PRS-1
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Above the scene editor, **Write Scene**, **Expand** and a **Length** choice (200, 500, 1000 or 2000 words) use the prose writer model from Settings. The length choice is remembered per user.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-PRS-2
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
**Write Scene** writes the selected scene from its brief, or from its chapter's brief if the scene has none; with neither, the user is told to create the structure from a plan or to draft the scene and use Expand.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-PRS-3
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Write Scene gives the model the synopsis, the act and chapter (titles and briefs), the scene's title and brief, the end of the nearest earlier scene that has text (up to 600 words), and the next scene's brief (or the next chapter's brief when the next scene starts a chapter and has none).
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-PRS-4
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
If the scene already has text, Write Scene asks before replacing it.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-PRS-5
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
**Expand** rewrites the selected text, or the whole scene if nothing is selected, as a longer passage of the chosen length; whitespace around the selection is left in place. The button reads **Expand Selection** when text is selected.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-PRS-6
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Expand gives the model the scene context of FR-PRS-3, up to 400 words before the passage, the passage, and only the opening of the text after it (whole sentences up to about 40 words), with the first following sentence named as the point to end before.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-PRS-7
PRIORITY: Should
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Expand refuses a passage that already has at least the chosen number of words.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-PRS-8
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
The model's text streams into the editor in place of the replaced text; the editor and the scene tree are locked while it writes, and **Stop** ends the request, keeping what was written.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-PRS-9
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Each generation is one undo step: Undo brings back exactly the text from before.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-PRS-10
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
A request is ended once the text passes the chosen length by a quarter (at least 50 words); the text is cut at the end of the last whole sentence within that limit and the status says so.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-PRS-11
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
When an expanded passage shares a run of five words with the text after it (checked against up to 300 following words), the request is ended and the passage is cut at the start of that sentence; a copy of the following text's first sentence at the very end is also removed. The whole answer is never removed.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-PRS-12
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Wrappers models add around prose are removed: code fences, a leading "Here is..." line, a leading heading or bold title, and section tags copied from the prompt (a leading tag is removed; anything from a later tag is dropped).
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-PRS-13
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
A failed request puts the original text back and shows the cause; an empty answer, or a length-limit stop before any text, is explained.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-PRS-14
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Without a prose writer model or a valid server address, the user is offered the Settings dialog.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-PRS-15
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Generated text is saved when the request ends, not while it streams; switching scenes or closing the application first stops the request, keeping what was written.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Spelling

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-SPL-1
PRIORITY: Should
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Spell checking uses Hunspell with the US English and UK English dictionaries shipped with the application (from LibreOffice's dictionaries; their licenses are shipped too). More languages can be added by putting a ``<code>.dic`` / ``<code>.aff`` pair into the user's dictionary folder (**Edit > Spelling > Open Dictionary Folder**). Dictionaries in folders with non-ASCII names work.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-SPL-2
PRIORITY: Should
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Words are runs of letters with apostrophes inside them ("don't", "Elin's"); curly apostrophes count as straight ones, the possessive "'s" of a known word is accepted, and letters joined to digits or ``_`` are not checked.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-SPL-3
PRIORITY: Should
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
**Add to Dictionary** puts a word into the user's personal dictionary, used for every language and novel and kept between sessions; **Ignore All** accepts a word until the program ends.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-SPL-4
PRIORITY: Should
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Misspelled words are underlined with a wavy line in the scene, synopsis and plan editors, in a colour readable in light and dark mode. Code (fenced blocks and code spans), link targets, URLs, e-mail addresses and tags are not checked.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-SPL-5
PRIORITY: Should
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Right-clicking a misspelled word offers up to 7 suggestions (replacing the word as one undo step; disabled while a model writes into the editor), **Add to Dictionary** and **Ignore All**, above the usual editing commands.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-SPL-6
PRIORITY: Should
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
**Edit > Spelling** turns checking on or off (saved per user) and chooses the dictionary. The dictionary is saved with the novel; a novel without a choice uses the last one chosen, else UK English where the computer's region is the UK, Ireland, Australia or New Zealand, and US English otherwise.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Synopsis

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-SYN-1
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
The Synopsis tab holds one synopsis per novel, saved automatically and byte for byte as written.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-SYN-2
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
A Planner model dropdown lists "Settings default" plus the server's models; a choice other than the default overrides Settings for this novel and is saved with it.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-SYN-3
PRIORITY: Should
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
A status line names the model plans will be generated with and reports when the model list cannot be fetched.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Plans

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-PLN-1
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
A novel can hold any number of plans, listed on the Plan tab with their title and generating model, or "(by hand)".
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-PLN-2
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
**+ New Plan** adds an empty plan to write by hand; **Delete** removes one after confirmation; double-click renames.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-PLN-3
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
The selected plan opens in an editor with Markdown highlighting and saves automatically.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-PLN-4
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Each generated plan records the model and the plan template that generated it; editing the plan by hand does not change that record.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Plan templates

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-TPL-1
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
The Synopsis tab offers a Plan template dropdown with a short description of the selected template; the choice is saved with the novel.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-TPL-2
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
**Three acts (25/50/25)**, the default: a plot-driven plan in three acts of about 25/50/25% of the chapters, with inciting incident, first plot point, midpoint, crisis, climax and resolution placed in their acts.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-TPL-3
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
**Inner stages across years**: a character-driven plan for a story spanning many years, built as the protagonist's progression through 5 to 8 named inner stages (in the manner of the stages of grief), each stage a part (``### Stage N``); every scene carries a concrete time marker instead of time-skip summaries, and the synopsis's ending is kept even when bleak or unresolved.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-TPL-4
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Every template produces chapters and scenes in the same notation, so any plan can drive Create Structure and the plan chat.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-TPL-5
PRIORITY: Should
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Adding a template requires no change outside the template list and its prompt.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Plan generation

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-GEN-1
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
**Generate Plan** sends the synopsis to the novel's planner model in the novel's plan template and always creates a new plan; existing plans are never replaced.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-GEN-2
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
The plan streams into the Plan tab as it is written, with a word count, and the Plan tab is brought forward.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-GEN-3
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Reasoning ("thinking") output is never shown in or saved with a plan.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-GEN-4
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
**Stop** ends generation and keeps the text written so far as the plan.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-GEN-5
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
A failed or empty generation adds no plan and shows the cause; a length-limit stop, including one spent entirely on reasoning, is explained with a remedy.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-GEN-6
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Generation requires a synopsis and a planner model; without a model the user is offered the Settings dialog.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-GEN-7
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
While a plan is generated, the plan list, plan editing and plan buttons are locked.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Plan chat

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-CHT-1
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
A chat box under the plan talks to the model that generated the selected plan, or to the novel's planner model for a hand-written plan; its header names the model.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-CHT-2
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Every request includes the synopsis, the current plan with the author's edits, and the conversation so far.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-CHT-3
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
A reply containing a revised plan replaces the plan as one undoable edit; a reply without one leaves the plan unchanged.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-CHT-4
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
A revised plan that was cut off is never applied; one that looks partial (fewer acts, under half the chapters or length) is applied only after confirmation.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-CHT-5
PRIORITY: Should
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Each plan keeps its own conversation across sessions; **Clear** forgets it after confirmation.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-CHT-6
PRIORITY: Should
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Enter sends, Shift+Enter adds a line; **Stop** aborts and returns the message to the input; plan editing is locked while a reply is written.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Structure from a plan

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-IMP-1
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
**Create Structure** builds acts, chapters and scenes from the selected plan's Structure section, without a model.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-IMP-2
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Structure lines are recognised as headings, bold lines, list items or plain ``Chapter N: Title`` lines, including typographic spaces and hyphens; act-level parts may be named Act, Stage, Part or Book and keep that word in their title; no structure line is ever taken into a brief.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-IMP-3
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Each act, chapter and scene receives its plan description as a brief; scene text starts empty.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-IMP-4
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
A new novel's untouched first scene is replaced silently; otherwise the user chooses to add after the manuscript or replace it, with a second confirmation stating the words that would be deleted.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-IMP-5
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
The structure is written in one atomic step; afterwards the Manuscript tab shows the first new scene. A plan with no structure produces an explanation and no change.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Book details

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-BOOK-1
PRIORITY: Should
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
**File > Novel Setup** edits the novel's book details for publishing, the fields Amazon KDP asks for when publishing an eBook: title, subtitle, series name and number, edition, language, primary author, contributors with their roles, description, up to 7 keywords, publishing rights (own copyright or public domain), whether it has sexually explicit content, reading age, up to 3 categories, publisher and ISBN. They are saved in the novel. A new novel opens the window right away, with the title suggested from its file name.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-BOOK-2
PRIORITY: Should
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
The window marks the details KDP requires (title, author, language, publishing rights, the sexually explicit content answer, a category) and lists, as they are edited, what is missing and what breaks KDP's rules: title and subtitle fewer than 200 characters together, a description of at most 4000 characters, series number and edition as digits only, a series number only with a series name, no HTML in titles, names or keywords, all contributors for a public domain work, a reading age range in order, and a well-formed ISBN. Saving is always possible, so details can be filled in over time. It notes that language, title, subtitle, edition and primary author can't be changed on KDP after publishing.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-BOOK-3
PRIORITY: Could
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
The window title shows the book's title when one is set, else the novel's file name.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Settings and models

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-SET-1
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Edit > Settings holds the LM Studio server address, default ``http://127.0.0.1:1234``; forms like ``127.0.0.1:1234`` or ``.../v1`` are accepted and invalid addresses are refused.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-SET-2
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
The dialog fetches the server's models when opened, when the address changes and on Refresh, leaving out embedding models and showing size, quantisation and loaded state.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-SET-3
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Separate dropdowns choose the orchestrator/planner model and the prose writer model; a saved model missing from the server stays selected and is flagged.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-SET-4
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Settings are stored per user, outside the novel.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Appearance

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-APP-1
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
**View > Theme** offers System, Light and Dark. The choice is saved per user and applied at startup.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-APP-2
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
System uses the platform's own style and colours, including its light or dark setting where it has one.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-APP-3
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Light and Dark use the same style and fixed palettes on every platform, and tell the platform which scheme is in use (so the window title bar matches where the platform supports it).
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-APP-4
PRIORITY: Should
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
**Switch Light/Dark** (Ctrl+Shift+D) switches from the scheme currently shown to the other one.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-APP-5
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Markdown highlighting uses colours that are readable on the current background and changes as soon as the theme does.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-APP-6
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Ctrl+Plus and Ctrl+Minus make the text of the scene, synopsis and plan editors larger and smaller in steps (8 to 48 pt; Ctrl+= also works without Shift), and Ctrl+0 restores the default of 12 pt. The same commands are in the View menu, disabled at the smallest and largest size. Headings scale with the text.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-APP-7
PRIORITY: Should
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
The text size is saved per user and applied at startup.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Markdown export and import

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-EXP-1
PRIORITY: Should
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
**Export as Markdown** writes the whole manuscript to one ``.md`` file: acts as ``#`` headings, chapters as ``##``, scenes separated by ``* * *``. Each act, chapter and scene is preceded by an HTML comment that Markdown viewers don't show (``<!-- act -->``, ``<!-- chapter -->``, ``<!-- scene: Title -->``), so the structure and scene titles can be rebuilt on import.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-EXP-2
PRIORITY: Should
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
**Export as EPUB** writes the manuscript as an EPUB 3 eBook: a title page (title, subtitle, series, author), one page per chapter headed by its title with a scene break between scenes, a part page for each act when the novel has more than one act, and a table of contents (chapters under their acts). The book gets a permanent identifier on its first export, kept with its details, so later exports are recognised as the same book. If the title, author or language is not set, the user is offered Novel Setup first.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-EXP-3
PRIORITY: Should
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
The EPUB's metadata comes from the book details: title and subtitle, author and contributors (with MARC relator roles), language as a BCP 47 code (``und`` if unknown), publisher, description, keywords as subjects, series with its number, and the ISBN. Without a title, the novel's file name is used.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-EXP-4
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
The EPUB is a valid container: ``mimetype`` is the first entry and stored uncompressed, all documents are well-formed XML (XHTML), and every file in the manifest is in the archive. Scene Markdown becomes XHTML; HTML written in a scene is shown as text, never interpreted.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-IMP-6
PRIORITY: Should
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
**Import from Markdown** creates a new novel from a Markdown file. From a NovelClanker export, all acts, chapters and scenes come back with their titles, order and text (only trailing blank lines of a scene are dropped); headings and ``* * *`` lines inside a scene's text stay part of the scene. Briefs, the synopsis and plans are not part of an export and start empty.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-IMP-7
PRIORITY: Should
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Markdown without NovelClanker's comments (an older export, or written by hand) is read by its headings: ``#`` starts an act, ``##`` a chapter, and a scene-break line (``* * *``, ``***``, ``---``) a new scene, named "Scene N". Text before the first heading goes into "Act 1" / "Chapter 1". Windows line ends and a UTF-8 byte order mark are accepted.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FR-IMP-8
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
The imported novel is written completely in one atomic step before it is opened; if that fails, nothing is left at the chosen location. An existing folder is never used, and a file without any text is refused.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Data and storage

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
A novel is one RocksDB database (format version 3) in which every scene's text is its own entry, so restructuring never rewrites prose.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: DAT-1
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Each scene's text is a separate entry (``scene/<id>/text``); acts and chapters store only a title, an optional brief and the ordered ids of their children.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: DAT-2
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Ids are unique across acts, chapters, scenes and plans and are never reused, even after deletion.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: DAT-3
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Every change that touches several entries (add, move, delete, structure import) is written atomically in one batch.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: DAT-4
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Every write is synced to disk before the application reports it saved.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: DAT-5
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
The synopsis, plans and scene text are stored byte for byte as given (including CR/LF, tabs, no-break spaces and NUL).
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: DAT-6
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Optional entries (synopsis, briefs, plan model override, plan template, plan chat) may be absent and read as empty, so adding them needs no format change.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: DAT-7
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
A new novel is written in format 1, the only format. A novel with any other format version is refused when opened, before anything is read from or written to it; it is never converted or guessed.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: DAT-8
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
A deleted node or plan leaves no entries behind (title, text, brief, chat).
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: DAT-9
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Application settings (server address, model choices, prose length, theme, text size, spell checking on/off and default dictionary, last novel) live in per-user settings, never in a novel.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: DAT-11
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Book details are optional entries ``novel/details/<name>`` (lower-case letters, digits and ``_``), stored byte for byte and written together in one atomic batch; an empty value removes the entry. New detail names need no format change.
<<<

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
.. list-table::
   :header-rows: 1

   * - Key
     - Value
   * - ``novel/format``, ``novel/next_id``
     - format version, next unused id
   * - ``novel/acts``; ``act/<id>/{title,chapters,brief}``
     - act order; act title, chapter order, brief
   * - ``chapter/<id>/{title,scenes,brief}``
     - chapter title, scene order, brief
   * - ``scene/<id>/{title,text,brief}``
     - scene title, Markdown text, brief
   * - ``novel/synopsis``, ``novel/plan_model``, ``novel/plan_template``
     - synopsis; planner model override; plan template
   * - ``novel/plans``; ``plan/<id>/{title,text,model,template,chat}``
     - plan order; plan title, Markdown, generating model, template, conversation (JSON)
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: AI integration

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The application talks only to the LM Studio server the user configures, over its HTTP APIs, and treats model output as untrusted text to be checked before it changes the novel.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: AI-1
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Models are listed from ``GET /api/v1/models``, falling back to ``/api/v0/models`` and then the OpenAI-compatible ``/v1/models`` when the server answers 404; connection errors are reported without fallback.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: AI-2
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Listing times out after 5 s with a clear message.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: AI-3
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Generation, chat and prose writing use the OpenAI-compatible ``POST /v1/chat/completions`` with streaming (server-sent events).
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: AI-4
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
A streaming request fails only after 5 minutes without data, allowing for model loading.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: AI-5
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Reasoning is removed whether it arrives in a separate channel (``reasoning_content``) or inline as ``<think>...</think>``, including blocks split across chunks or opened by the chat template.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: AI-6
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Each plan template has its own prompt asking for a fixed Markdown shape: premise, characters and settings, a planning section written first (a story spine of turning points, or a stage progression), a Structure section of parts, ``#### Chapter`` and ``- **Scene N: Title**`` lines, and open questions. Every prompt demands faithfulness to the synopsis, no padding, and the synopsis's language.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: AI-7
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
The chat prompt requires the complete revised plan between ``<plan>`` and ``</plan>`` for changes, keeping the plan's own part headings (acts or stages), and a plain answer for questions; the newest message carries a reminder to return the whole plan.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: AI-8
PRIORITY: Should
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Earlier revised plans are not resent in chat history; the current plan in the system prompt replaces them.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: AI-9
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
A response stopped by the length limit is reported as truncated (``finish_reason: length``); errors inside the stream or in HTTP error bodies are shown to the user.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: AI-10
PRIORITY: Could
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Sampling temperature is 0.6 for plan generation, 0.5 for plan chat and 0.8 for prose writing.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: AI-11
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
The prose prompts give each part of the context as its own tagged section (synopsis, act, chapter, scene, previous text, next scene brief; for Expand also text before, passage, text after) and leave out empty ones.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: AI-12
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
The prose prompts require finished prose in the surrounding point of view, tense, voice and language; faithfulness to the brief and synopsis; following the instructions closely and stopping once they are covered, with no foreshadowing, summary, reflection or imagined ending beyond them; and plain text only (no Markdown, HTML, code blocks, title, introduction or notes).
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: AI-13
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
The prose prompts end with the length as a maximum, with a paragraph estimate. Write Scene may stop early once the scene is covered; Expand must keep every event of the passage in order, keep the author's dialogue, add detail rather than plot, and never continue past the passage.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Non-functional requirements

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The application must never lose or silently alter the author's text, must keep working when the model server is slow or wrong, and must build and run without special steps.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-1
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
AREA: Reliability
STATEMENT: >>>
No user action or model reply discards saved text without an explicit confirmation or a single undo step bringing it back; destructive confirmations state what will be lost.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-2
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
AREA: Reliability
STATEMENT: >>>
While a model is writing, the text it may replace is locked against edits.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-3
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
AREA: Reliability
STATEMENT: >>>
A slow, unreachable or misbehaving server never blocks the UI; every request can be stopped, and a model that does not stop on its own is stopped by the application (FR-PRS-10).
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-4
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
AREA: Reliability
STATEMENT: >>>
Closing the application or switching novels saves pending edits and stops running requests (keeping partial plans and prose as Stop does).
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-5
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
AREA: Platforms
STATEMENT: >>>
The same code builds on Windows, Linux and macOS with Conan and CMake; Windows is the tested platform.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-6
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
AREA: Deployment
STATEMENT: >>>
On Windows each build copies the Qt runtime next to the executable so it starts by double-click; ``cmake --install`` produces a self-contained folder including the MSVC runtime. On Linux ``cmake --install`` produces a self-contained folder with the Qt libraries and plugins, relying only on the desktop's system libraries. ``build.bat`` (Windows) and ``build.sh`` (Linux) run every step from dependencies to that folder.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-7
PRIORITY: Should
STATUS: Implemented
AREA: Build
STATEMENT: >>>
A project profile (``profiles/windows-msvc``) fixes compiler, C++20, Ninja and RocksDB's UTF-8 file names; the build has no compiler warnings at ``/W4``.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-8
PRIORITY: Should
STATUS: Implemented
AREA: Performance
STATEMENT: >>>
Typing is never delayed by saving; saves happen at most every 1.5 s of idle time per editor. Streaming views refresh at most every 100 ms.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-9
PRIORITY: Should
STATUS: Implemented
AREA: Usability
STATEMENT: >>>
All user-visible strings go through Qt translation (``tr``).
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-10
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
AREA: Privacy
STATEMENT: >>>
Novel text is sent only to the configured LM Studio server; the application has no telemetry and needs no account.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-11
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
AREA: Testability
STATEMENT: >>>
Storage, model client, chat, plan parsing, prose prompts and the main window are covered by seven CTest suites that run without a real server (fake HTTP server) and without showing windows (Qt ``minimal`` platform).
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-12
PRIORITY: Should
STATUS: Implemented
AREA: Testability
STATEMENT: >>>
Optional live checks run the same code against a real LM Studio when environment variables are set, including prose length and repeat checks.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-13
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
AREA: Licensing
STATEMENT: >>>
NovelClanker is licensed under GPL-3.0-or-later (``LICENSE``). Every self-contained folder (``cmake --install``) contains ``LICENSE.txt``, a ``THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt`` listing each bundled library with its version, license and homepage, and each library's license files in ``licenses/<name>/``, including libraries built into Qt's shared libraries. Qt stays a set of replaceable shared libraries (LGPL-3.0).
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Constraints, open issues and traceability

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
**Constraints and assumptions.**
<<<

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
- One author per novel; RocksDB's lock makes concurrent access impossible by design.
- LM Studio is installed and its server is running; the application does not start, load or unload models.
- Plan quality depends on the chosen model. In testing, ``openai_gpt-oss-20b`` produced the best plans, and models with a context below 32k tokens could not hold a full plan.
- Prose quality and length discipline depend on the chosen model; small models count words poorly and may not stop on their own, which FR-PRS-10 and FR-PRS-11 guard against.
- Qt and RocksDB are built from source on first install, which can take an hour or more.
<<<

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
**Planned and open items.**
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: PLN-1
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Implemented
STATEMENT: >>>
Prose writer: write a scene from its brief and the preceding text, and expand passages, with the prose writer model (FR-PRS).
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: PLN-2
PRIORITY: Should
STATUS: Planned
STATEMENT: >>>
Edit briefs in the UI (the store already supports it); needed for Write Scene on scenes added by hand.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: PLN-3
PRIORITY: Should
STATUS: Planned
STATEMENT: >>>
Option to turn off or limit reasoning for reasoning models, which can exhaust their context before answering.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: PLN-4
PRIORITY: Should
STATUS: Planned
STATEMENT: >>>
Test on macOS, and package for Linux distributions (e.g. AppImage). Building and testing on Linux (Ubuntu 22.04) is done.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: PLN-5
PRIORITY: Could
STATUS: Planned
STATEMENT: >>>
Renumber chapters after a model inserts one (models write "8.1" or "11A").
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: PLN-6
PRIORITY: Could
STATUS: Planned
STATEMENT: >>>
Undo for Create Structure when it replaces the manuscript.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: PLN-7
PRIORITY: Could
STATUS: Planned
STATEMENT: >>>
Per-novel prose writer model override, like the planner model on the Synopsis tab.
<<<

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
**Traceability.**
<<<

